/**
 * \brief Take the exclusive frame lock of a super-chunk and hold it across
 * several operations.
 *
 * When the (opt-in) file locking is enabled on the handle (see the {@code locking}
 * member of #blosc2_stdio_params, or the BLOSC_LOCKING environment variable),
 * every schunk operation locks and unlocks the on-disk frame individually, so
 * a sequence of operations is not atomic as a whole.  Bracketing the sequence
 * between blosc2_schunk_lock() and blosc2_schunk_unlock() makes it atomic
 * against other handles and processes: the operations inside nest on the
 * already-held lock instead of re-acquiring it.
 *
 * Everything inside the bracket is serialized exclusively — including plain
 * reads through other locked handles — so keep brackets short.  The bracket
 * is re-entrant on the same handle.  When locking is not enabled on the
 * handle (or the super-chunk is not disk-based), this is a no-op returning
 * success, so callers do not need to check whether locking is active.
 *
 * @param schunk The super-chunk.
 *
 * @return 0 on success; a negative error code (e.g. BLOSC2_ERROR_LOCK)
 * otherwise.
 */
public static native int blosc2_schunk_lock(blosc2_schunk schunk);

/**
 * \brief Release the frame lock taken with blosc2_schunk_lock().
 *
 * @param schunk The super-chunk.
 *
 * @return 0 on success; a negative error code otherwise.  Like
 * blosc2_schunk_lock(), a no-op when locking is not enabled.
 */
public static native int blosc2_schunk_unlock(blosc2_schunk schunk);

/**
 * \brief Start a sequence of chunk updates of a super-chunk.
 *
 * A contiguous frame keeps its chunks back to back, so an update that changes
 * the compressed size of a chunk moves everything stored behind it: updating
 * all n chunks one by one moves ~n^2/2 chunks.  Between
 * blosc2_schunk_begin_updates() and blosc2_schunk_end_updates(), such an
 * update moves nothing else.  The chunk goes into its old place when it fits
 * there (together with the free space right before and after it), else to the
 * end of the frame, and the space left over stays free until
 * blosc2_schunk_end_updates() closes all of it in one pass, in which every
 * chunk moves at most once.  The frame is valid at every point in between,
 * just larger, and {@code cbytes} includes the free space until the end.
 *
 * Writing functions that update several chunks (b2nd_set_slice_cbuffer(),
 * b2nd_set_orthogonal_selection(), b2nd_resize(),
 * blosc2_schunk_set_slice_buffer()) do this by themselves; use it to bracket
 * a sequence of such calls or of blosc2_schunk_update_chunk() calls.
 *
 * It also takes the exclusive frame lock, as blosc2_schunk_lock() does, until
 * blosc2_schunk_end_updates().  Brackets nest.  For super-chunks that are not
 * contiguous frames this is just the lock (a no-op without locking).  A frame
 * whose bracket is never closed stays valid; its free space just stays, like
 * the space of deleted chunks.
 *
 * @param schunk The super-chunk.
 *
 * @return 0 on success; a negative error code otherwise (then nothing has started).
 */
public static native int blosc2_schunk_begin_updates(blosc2_schunk schunk);

/**
 * \brief End a sequence of chunk updates started with blosc2_schunk_begin_updates().
 *
 * The outermost call closes the free space that the updates left in a
 * contiguous frame (moving each chunk at most once; a frame on disk is cut to
 * its new length) and releases the lock.
 *
 * @param schunk The super-chunk.
 *
 * @return 0 on success; a negative error code otherwise.  The lock is released
 * either way.
 */
public static native int blosc2_schunk_end_updates(blosc2_schunk schunk);

/**
 * \brief Re-sync the cached counters (nchunks, nbytes, cbytes) and
 * metalayers of a disk-based super-chunk when another handle — possibly in
 * another process — has changed it.
 *
 * This gives a deterministic sync point for readers in a single-writer,
 * multiple-readers (SWMR) workflow: after calling it, the counters in
 * \p schunk reflect the current on-disk state.  Data-access functions
 * already do this implicitly, so calling it is only needed to observe
 * changes without touching data (e.g. when polling).
 *
 * A no-op for super-chunks not backed by an on-disk frame.
 *
 * @param schunk The super-chunk to refresh.
 *
 * @return 1 if the counters were re-synced, 0 if they were already current,
 * or a negative error code.
 */
public static native int blosc2_schunk_refresh(blosc2_schunk schunk);

